## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
A practical dynamic 2x2 submatrix counter seizes local updates; analytic challenge: bound work per update and handle edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Local neighborhood counting; incremental updates
- Amortized O(B) per batch; worst-case O(B) with 4 checks per update
- Space: store full matrix and current count; avoid full scan

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateCell(mat, n, i, j, val, cur) {
  const old = mat[i][j];
  if (old === val) return cur;
  for (let di = -1; di <= 0; di++) {
    for (let dj = -1; dj <= 0; dj++) {
      const r = i + di, c = j + dj;
      if (r >= 0 && c >= 0 && r + 1 < n && c + 1 < n) {
        const before = mat[r][c] & mat[r+1][c] & mat[r][c+1] & mat[r+1][c+1];
        // after flip not yet applied
        // compute new values after applying val to mat[i][j]
        const a = (r === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r][c];
        const b = (r+1 === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c];
        const c2 = (r === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r][c+1];
        const d = (r+1 === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c+1];
        const after = a & b & c2 & d;
        if (before !== after) {
          cur += (after ? 1 : -1) * 1; // adjust
        }
      }
    }
  }
  mat[i][j] = val;
  return cur;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to counting 3x3 all-ones squares with O(1) per update? 
- How do you handle non-binary updates or weighted counts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis under streaming appends with cache considerations. It evaluates understanding of how to design data structures that balance update frequency, query latency, and memory access patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding windows**: Maintaining a fixed-size view over streaming data
- **Circular buffers**: O(1) rotation without shifting elements
- **Amortized analysis**: Batched updates spread costs over time
- **Cache-friendly layout**: Sequential memory access patterns for spatial locality

## Code Example
```javascript
class StreamingAnalyticsStore {
  constructor(W, kQueries, mKeys) {
    this.W = W;
    this.blocks = [];
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.windowSum = 0;
    this.queryRanges = kQueries.map(([start, end]) => ({ start, end }));
    this.keySet = new Set(mKeys);
  }

  appendBatch(newBlocks) {
    for (const block of newBlocks) {
      if (this.size === this.W) {
        this.windowSum -= this.blocks[this.head];
      }
      this.blocks[this.head] = block;
      this.windowSum += block;
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
      this.size = Math.min(this.size + 1, this.W);
    }
  }

  rangeSum(queryIndex) {
    const { start, end } = this.queryRanges[queryIndex];
    const windowSize = this.size;
    const startIdx = (this.head - end + this.W) % this.W;
    const endIdx = (this.head - start - 1 + this.W) % this.W;
    let sum = 0;
    let i = startIdx;
    while (i !== (endIdx + 1) % this.W) {
      sum += this.blocks[i];
      i = (i + 1) % this.W;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  hasKey(key) {
    return this.keySet.has(key);
  }
}
```

